It chooses the quote with the highest expected value, or does not respond if all quotes have negative value. After the result and later markout are observed, that RFQ becomes another training example. Thus, the model does not separately predict a fill probability and then assume your original theoretical edge survives; it learns both probability and realized edge from the same win event. Your original framework already supplies the contextual fill-curve and risk inputs, while the hit-ratio paper supplies the control logic for converting those learned inputs into state-dependent quotes.
